<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bevagna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mevania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bevagna, the ancient Mevania, is a small town in the Umbrian plain of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the route of the Roman Via Flaminia. An Umbrian community and then a Roman municipium, it preserves a Roman mosaic of sea creatures and the remains of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. It later came under papal rule and keeps much of its medieval centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bevagna.jpg" alt="Bevagna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BevagnaDec122023_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bevagna lies in the flood plain of the Topino river, in the western part of the Umbrian valley and about 225 metres above sea level. It is 25 km south-east of Perugia, 8 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foligno">Foligno</a> and 16 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>. The Clitunno runs close to its ancient walls, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timia">Timia</a> waters a fertile, well-cultivated plain. In antiquity Classical authors, among them Columella, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a> and Lucan, refer to the fertility of this valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As Mevania the town was an important Umbrian city on the Via Flaminia, between Carsulae and Fulginium. It was already a significant centre before the Roman conquest, and Umbrian forces were stationed there in 308 BCE when the consul Fabius advanced against them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> lists it among the strongest Umbrian cities, and Pliny records that it was enclosed by brick walls and that its territory produced a prized wine called Irtia. Inscriptions show that it belonged to the tribus Aemilia and had quattuorviri among its magistrates.</p><p>The city suffered serious damage in barbarian incursions in the 6th century. After the 11th century it became a free municipality governed by consuls, and in 1249 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> granted it the right to choose its chief magistrate. In November of that year the imperial vicar Thomas I of Aquino besieged and badly damaged it, in the context of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>'s claim to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>.</p><p>It was under the Trinci family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foligno">Foligno</a> from 1371 to 1439, when Cardinal Giovanni Vitelleschi returned it to the direct rule of the Holy See. From 1562 to 1566 its governor was Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Charles Borromeo</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> of 1798 Bevagna was assigned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Spello. The papal government was restored on 14 May 1814, Pope Leo XII granted the town the title of city in 1825, and in 1860 it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Roman remains include a temple of the 2nd century, converted into the church of the Madonna della Neve, and the arcaded substructures of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the 2nd century, which shaped the later streets. A bath building preserves a floor mosaic in black and white of the same century, showing tritons, hippocamps, octopuses, dolphins and lobsters.</p><p>The medieval square holds the church of San Michele Arcangelo, with a façade of the early 13th century, the Romanesque church of San Silvestro, built by the architect Binello, whose plaque is dated 1195, and the Palazzo dei Consoli of the later 13th century, which has housed the Teatro Torti since 1886. Medieval festivals are held each year, including the Mercato delle Gaite in June, in which four quarters of the town compete.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bevagna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Umbrian communities were independent towns and hill sanctuaries, among them Iguvium, Tuder, Asisium, <b>Mevania</b>, Spoletium and Ameria, without a lasting central government.</blockquote><a href="/state/umbrians">Umbrians</a></li></ul></section>
